#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153168} :activity/id #uuid "2d6eb053-cae1-5c17-b076-ba71324db2b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153410 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be an  $m \\times m$ matrix. Recall that a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}_1.\n$$\n\nWe can write this equation as \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ maps the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ to the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNow suppose that there exists a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v_2$ that is mapped to the zero vector in *two* steps by the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ That is,\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_2 = \\mathbf v_1 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad (A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_1 = \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda.$\n\nWe can represent this schematically as a chain of mappings, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I}  \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWe call $\\mathbf v_2$ a **generalized eigenvector of rank** $2$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in two steps. \n\nBy the same idea, an eigenvector of $A$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in one step." :tutorial/id #uuid "88994c46-d16a-531c-895a-e2c3fdddfc1f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5419 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70c56d2e-edef-5c66-b434-a79d78754fe4" :step/math-academy-id 33794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152034 :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050857}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3ba3a9ff-1652-5e2f-a17b-31f53f5fa7ce" :step/math-academy-id 33795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152035 :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"} :step/id #uuid "4c7afd25-604b-599f-93ac-e074a6562587" :step/math-academy-id 33799 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152036 :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362682}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c580f07b-112f-562a-a89e-5d7287e732f7" :step/math-academy-id 33800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153411 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf v,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ has only one eigenvalue, namely $\\lambda = 3.$ Let's show that $\\mathbf v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to this eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we compute $A-3I{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-3I{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs we can see,  $A-3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$ of $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "398d8883-81a4-5080-8b00-904f74cdef07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5587 :tutorial/title "An Example of a Generalized Eigenvector of Rank Two for a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "3ec840df-f049-5126-988c-9c7bbb99172d" :step/math-academy-id 34485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153412 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be an  $m \\times m$ matrix. A **generalized eigenvector of rank $n$** corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}_n$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps." :tutorial/id #uuid "6382bc76-a365-5639-83d8-91bd8605500a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5588 :tutorial/title "Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank N"} :step/id #uuid "cd7d895d-dd6d-5fb6-acf9-cde71c0edb5f" :step/math-academy-id 34486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153170}}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152358}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413002} :activity/id #uuid "b5cf7ec7-b0e0-5d92-bb87-56ae58980cda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408 :topic/id #uuid "f1e8e720-50ea-5aa2-9e19-afac3ed8baad" :topic/math-academy-id 2738} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "551aa92b-df71-5900-93f2-5ef5c2f52582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412983} {:answer/id #uuid "57f29804-0dce-5ac6-8836-fe816e8c4d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412984} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0d3c84-3d53-59fb-b4db-6527f0bb894b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412985} {:answer/id #uuid "7b712b8f-0b0e-5fc8-bf18-7ff7f27b35bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412986} {:answer/id #uuid "ed537725-596f-59c3-9e2a-8cf97c17101c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412993}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412986} :answer-field/id #uuid "f812c18b-3e31-5102-8677-d82c1a372eb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddb46c6-735a-5bdf-b2a2-b57344c5441a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109434" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}s\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-4I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $s,u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $s$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\ns\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }u\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $s$ to $0$ in $4$ steps, \nthe vector $s$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $4$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A=A-0I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2bc8515b-cb7e-52c2-b2ca-3e1502bf57b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4520b82c-7699-585f-98d4-b020b73654d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412982} {:answer/id #uuid "9865884d-6f2a-52d2-8326-716abac188bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412987} {:answer/id #uuid "bb216ff2-4b67-5cf4-8b40-eae9eda5614c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412988} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e55130-8888-5317-8d53-8e631e8c4625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412991} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4717c9-e5b3-5af6-a5a6-f49fdfc568d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412991} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1915239-194e-5aaa-b92c-9c8908387454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28941d92-3462-55d4-9376-780e240021c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-109277" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ad6297d7-7ae6-5be0-a908-e7a0cc46d152" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3927cd36-e744-5c4b-b518-90f4314da95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412980} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0f7a4e-e2de-5221-a243-87d47db94856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412981} {:answer/id #uuid "be644adc-7532-5ed2-b8e4-b29d16f2b2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412989} {:answer/id #uuid "c05a3db1-b2e4-555c-b799-5b9528462b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412990} {:answer/id #uuid "e77d1188-387d-5333-b099-521dba1d6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412992} :answer-field/id #uuid "d49a1696-245b-5c6e-b7fe-d2569b597279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e5966d3-ce4d-5835-ab91-1f478e587602" :question/math-academy-id "q-109510" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ea9a4e19-af76-5650-86a8-7d1214ec77ba"}] :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412979}]}
